{"id":1623,"date":"2020-12-03T13:05:31","date_gmt":"2020-12-03T21:05:31","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1623"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:38:33","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:38:33","slug":"codrone-blockly-sr-flight-events","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-blockly-sr-flight-events\/","title":{"rendered":"Flight Events"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215500d8df\" name=\"6a2215500d8df\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Flight events<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Out of all of the menus in Blockly, the one you will be using the most is flight commands. This is where you will find blocks to have your CoDrone fly in different directions and, probably most important, take off and land! To find the takeoff() and land() blocks, click on the flight commands menu. The takeoff() and land() blocks should be at the top! <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1624&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215500dd76\" name=\"6a2215500dd76\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Take off<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Any code that has your CoDrone in the air will start with the takeoff() block. This will make your drone launch from the ground and fly vertically in the air. Your CoDrone should then hover for about three seconds before moving on to the next block in your code.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Let\u2019s make your CoDrone take off now! Drag the takeoff() block from the flight commands menu to your workspace. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1625&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215500dde6\" name=\"6a2215500dde6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Hover<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If you want your CoDrone to hover for more than three seconds, there is a way to do that! Find the hover (_ sec) block in the flight commands menu and connect it to the bottom of the takeoff() block. Next, type in the number of seconds you would like your CoDrone to hover for. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1626&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215500de35\" name=\"6a2215500de35\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Land<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The land() block brings your CoDrone to the ground gently. When you\u2019re finished with everything else in your code, attach the land() block to the bottom of your program. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1628&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215500de93\" name=\"6a2215500de93\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Emergency stop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">There might be times when your CoDrone is about to crash into something and you need to have it make an emergency stop before it does serious damage. If you want your CoDrone\u2019s motors to stop without crashing into the ceiling first, program a kill switch by dragging a rounded when _ key press block from the gray logic\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">menu into the workspace. This should be the block that doesn\u2019t have a connector on the side! Pick the key you would like to use to stop your drone from the block\u2019s drop-down menu, but as a warning, using the backspace key can sometimes delete blocks of code!\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Next, go to the flight commands menu, find the emergency_stop() block, and drag it into the when _ \u00a0key press block.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Note: this will be a separate program but will be in the same workspace! <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1629&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215500dee0\" name=\"6a2215500dee0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Your workspace should look something like the picture. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1630&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Want to see what it looks like in a text-based coding language? Click either the Java, Python, or Arduino icon on the right-hand side of your workspace to take a glimpse into your programming future!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1631&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215500df50\" name=\"6a2215500df50\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: CoDrone Pushups\u00a0<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Try to make your CoDrone take off and land 5 times in a row with one code! Currently, your CoDrone can do one pushup with the code you have.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><b>Rules:\u00a0<\/b><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> The CoDrone must completely leave the ground on takeoff.\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> The CoDrone must be completely back on the ground on landing.\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> Don&#8217;t forget an emergency stop!\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">See who can do the most CoDrone pushups in 10 seconds OR who can use the least amount of blocks in their code!\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 Flight events [vc_column_text]Out of all of the menus in Blockly, the one you will be using the most is flight commands. This is where you will find&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-blockly-sr-flight-events\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1753,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":806,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1817,1763,1777,1793,626,1533,1438,853,144,167,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2108,2164,2112,2122,2126],"class_list":["post-1623","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-flight-events","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-senior","module-senior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1623","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":5,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1623\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2449,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1623\/revisions\/2449"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1753"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1623"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1623"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1623"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}